## The V-Cut French Tip: 2026's Defining Stiletto Silhouette

A curved smile line was built for a rounded nail. It has no reason to work on a point, and mostly, it does not. A V-cut french tip is a smile line that angles to a peak at the center of the nail instead of sweeping in a curve, and on a stiletto shape, that peak lines up with the nail's own taper. The two angles echo each other instead of fighting. That is the actual mechanism competitors skip when they just say "V-cut is trending": the geometry matches, so the eye reads it as intentional rather than approximate.

This is the look most nail techs mean when a client says <strong>stiletto french tip nails</strong> without further detail, and the V-cut has become the 2026 baseline the way the classic curved smile line was the baseline for almond and oval shapes. The white tip stays crisp against a sheer nude or barely-pink base, and the point of the V typically lands just below the nail's own apex, leaving enough space for the shape to still register as stiletto rather than just "pointy."

Depth is the main variable. A shallow V reads closer to a traditional French with a slight edge. A deep V, cutting nearly to the halfway mark of the nail, turns the whole look sharper and more graphic, closer to what shows up in editorial nail work. Neither is more correct. A deep V wants length to actually show its shape, which is worth knowing before you book the appointment. For a broader look at <a href='https://mirelleinspo.com/blog/french-nails/french-tip-nail-designs'>how deep French tips work across every nail shape, stiletto included</a>, the V-cut technique extends well past this one shape, but stiletto is where it makes the most visual sense.

