## Micro French Tips: The Minimalist Take

A skinny line does more than a wide one, at least on this shape. That is the whole logic behind the micro french tip. What's the difference between a micro french tip and a classic french tip? A micro french tip uses a noticeably thinner line, typically a millimeter or two rather than the wider band of a classic French, positioned right at the very edge of the nail rather than covering a larger portion of the tip.

On a stiletto point, this restraint reads as deliberate rather than plain. The thin line traces the shape without adding visual weight to an already elongated silhouette, which is part of why "so sharp" comes up so often in how people describe this pairing specifically. It also happens to be one of the more forgiving stiletto French variations to maintain, since less of the design is exposed to daily wear and chipping at the very tip.

Micro tips work in white, but they have expanded well past that. A hairline of black, a thread of gold, or even a barely-there nude-on-nude line all fall under this same minimalist category, and all of them let a sheer, glossy base do most of the visual work. This is the version to reach for when the goal is clean lines over a statement look, or when a shorter stiletto length does not have enough surface for a deeper V-cut to read clearly.

