## How to Get a Clean French Tip on a Pointed Nail

How do you do a french tip on stiletto nails? The short answer: nail guides placed to follow the point rather than a curve, a steady hand or airbrush for the line itself, and a longer cure or dry time before topcoat since any smudge on a narrow tip is far more visible than the same smudge on a wider, rounded one. It is a more exacting version of a familiar technique, not an entirely different skill.

The smile line is the term for the visible boundary between the base color and the tip color on any French manicure, and on a stiletto shape, that boundary has to be planned before a single stroke goes down. Most techs use adhesive nail guides angled to match the nail's own taper, applying them slightly higher on the sides than they would on a round or almond shape to account for how narrow the tip surface actually is toward the point.

